Subject: The profile records the call a `f.apply(this, arguments)` site makes

What this does
  - At the `apply_arguments` opcode, in both the threaded and the monolith interpreter, the
    profiler records a call site at the opcode: the value the site calls (`f`, the register the
    `apply` is taken from) and the argument count, so the callee is the forwarded-to function and
    not the `apply` function itself. The profiler only; no generated-code layout and no
    interpreter behaviour changes.

Why we need it
  `apply_arguments` is how a `Class.create`-style forwarder passes its arguments on, and the
  profile is the only thing the generator has to learn what such a site calls. It named no callee
  there, so the generator could not see which functions such a site forwards to.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -30363,0 +30364,4 @@
+        /* The call this site forwards to, recorded like any other call site's:
+           the target is the value the site calls, not the `apply` function. */
+        AOT_PROF_CALL(ctx, f->b, (uint32_t)(ip - f->b->regvm_code) + 7, sp[-3],
+                      f->argc);
@@ -36155,0 +36160,5 @@
+                    /* The call this site forwards to, recorded like any other
+                       call site's: the target is the value the site calls, not
+                       the `apply` function. */
+                    AOT_PROF_CALL(ctx, b, (uint32_t)(rpc - b->regvm_code), sp[-3],
+                                  argc);
